{
  "slug": "heap-sort",
  "title": "Heap Sort",
  "summary": "Build a max heap, then repeatedly move its maximum into a growing sorted suffix.",
  "usage": "How it works\nA max heap stores each parent >= its children. In a zero-based array, children are 2*r+1 and 2*r+2. Build the heap bottom-up in O(n), swap its root with the last heap value, shrink the heap, and sift down. Useful when worst-case bounds and constant auxiliary memory matter. The visualization shows the heap in array form, not as a tree.\n\nProperties and prerequisites\nStable: no. In-place: yes; sift-down is iterative.\n\nComplexity\nTime: O(n log n) worst/average; O(n) best with all equal values. Space: O(1).\n\nExample\nInput: [5, 2, 4, 1, 3]. Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].\n\nTry it\nUse Play/Pause, Previous/Next, the timeline, and playback speed. Answer the prediction checkpoints to continue; feedback reveals the expected answer. Python is the line-synchronized walkthrough; Java is an equivalent complete implementation. Sorting functions accept empty arrays, duplicates, negative values, and already sorted inputs. Java methods belong inside a class; Python functions can be called directly.",
  "category": "Sorting",
  "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
  "visualizer": "ARRAY",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n) worst/average; O(n) best with all equal values",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "pythonSource": "def heap_sort(a):\n    def sift(root, end):\n        while 2 * root + 1 < end:\n            child = 2 * root + 1  # Left child in the array-backed max heap.\n            if child + 1 < end and a[child + 1] > a[child]:\n                child += 1\n            if a[root] >= a[child]:  # The heap property is already satisfied.\n                break\n            a[root], a[child] = a[child], a[root]  # Move the larger child upward.\n            root = child\n    for start in range(len(a) // 2 - 1, -1, -1):\n        sift(start, len(a))  # Build the max heap bottom-up.\n    for end in range(len(a) - 1, 0, -1):\n        a[0], a[end] = a[end], a[0]  # Extract the maximum into the sorted suffix.\n        sift(0, end)\n    return a\n",
  "javaSource": "static void heapSort(int[] a) {\n    for (int start = a.length / 2 - 1; start >= 0; start--)\n        sift(a, start, a.length);\n    for (int end = a.length - 1; end > 0; end--) {\n        int t = a[0]; a[0] = a[end]; a[end] = t;\n        sift(a, 0, end);\n    }\n}\nstatic void sift(int[] a, int root, int end) {\n    while (2 * root + 1 < end) {\n        int child = 2 * root + 1;\n        if (child + 1 < end && a[child + 1] > a[child]) child++;\n        if (a[root] >= a[child]) break;\n        int t = a[root]; a[root] = a[child]; a[child] = t;\n        root = child;\n    }\n}",
